/*!
    \page index.html
    \title Build CarLog with Felgo

    How much have you spent on fuel this month? Build CarLog with Felgo and QML
    to register a car, keep its fuel records, and compare spending by date.

    \raw HTML
    <div class="screenshot-grid">
      <figure><a href="images/home.png"><img src="images/home.png" alt="CarLog Home with a Tesla vehicle card and two fuel records"></a><figcaption>Home shows the saved car and fuel records. Tap a record to edit it.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><a href="images/statistics.png"><img src="images/statistics.png" alt="CarLog Statistics showing two records, 67.50 liters and 120.90 euros"></a><figcaption>Statistics uses the same records, filtered by date.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    \endraw

    \section1 What you will build

    You will connect the app's data model, build the onboarding controls,
    create a reusable record list and fuel form, and add validation and storage.
    The last steps calculate period totals and connect the circular page transition.

    Use Felgo with Qt 6.8.3. This guide assumes basic QML properties, signals,
    layouts, and JavaScript functions. It starts with a runnable app shell:
    the theme, vehicle card, date picker, navigation container, and animation
    components are supplied. We will build the fields, list delegate, and
    behavior that make those pieces work together.

    \raw HTML
    <p>Download <a href="downloads/carlog-starter.zip">the starter project</a> and unzip it into a new folder. Use <a href="downloads/carlog-solution.zip">the completed project</a> to check your work.</p>
    \endraw

    When changing a function or component below, replace its existing version.
    Keep the supplied imports, IDs, and surrounding layout unless the step
    says to change them: other bindings refer to those names.

    \section1 1. Connect the app's data model

    A button, a saved value, and a page transition have different jobs.
    CarLog separates them so the same records can appear on both tabs:

    \table
      \header \li File \li Responsibility
      \row \li qml/Main.qml \li Owns Storage, Logic, DataModel, and the single NavigationStack.
      \row \li qml/logic/Logic.qml \li Declares the actions a page can request.
      \row \li qml/model/DataModel.qml \li Validates changes, writes Storage, and exposes values to the pages.
      \row \li qml/pages/ \li Collects input and displays model properties; requests actions through signals.
    \endtable

    Open Logic.qml. Its signals name the actions the app understands:
    \quotefile qml/logic/Logic.qml

    Signals do not save anything by themselves. DataModel listens to this
    Logic object through its dispatcher property. In Main.qml, replace the
    DataModel instance with:
    \quotefromfile qml/Main.qml
    \skipto DataModel {
    \printuntil /^  \}/

    For example, the form will call logic.addFuelRecord(draft). DataModel's
    matching onAddFuelRecord handler will process the draft. When the model
    assigns a new fuelRecords array, the lists bound to that property update.
    The page does not need to reload its list manually.

    Main creates these shared objects above the pages, making their IDs
    available in the pages' QML creation context. Keep storage, logic, and
    dataModel in Main; do not create another Storage object inside each page.
    The downloads already give Storage its own tutorial database.

    Run the app. The welcome heading and car should appear. There is no name
    field yet. In the next step, we will give that screen something to submit.

    \section1 2. Build the car registration controls

    In qml/pages/OnboardingPage.qml, add this code below the car image,
    inside the Column:
    \quotefile docs/snippets/onboarding-controls.inc

    RowLayout places the label beside the field; Layout.fillWidth lets the
    field use the remaining width. The enabled binding follows the trimmed
    input, so the button stays disabled for an empty name or spaces.
    Pressing it emits onboardingFinished. Main forwards that request to Logic,
    and the model changes onboardingCompleted to open Home.

    Try spaces, then enter Tesla and press Register your car. Home should open.
    This first version only keeps registration in memory. Restart the app
    to return to onboarding before continuing.

    \raw HTML
    <figure class="screenshot"><a href="images/onboarding.png"><img src="images/onboarding.png" alt="Car name field and disabled registration button"></a><figcaption>The enabled binding prevents registration with an empty name.</figcaption></figure>
    \endraw

    \section1 3. Keep the car name after a restart

    In qml/model/DataModel.qml, update completeOnboarding(name):
    \quotefromfile qml/model/DataModel.qml
    \skipto function completeOnboarding(
    \printuntil /^  \}/

    Save the name and flag before changing onboardingCompleted in memory.
    Main listens to that property to decide when to open MainPage. Updating
    it last means the next screen sees the registered name.

    In the same file, update prepare() to restore the saved state:
    \quotefromfile qml/model/DataModel.qml
    \skipto function prepare(
    \printuntil /^  \}/

    prepare() runs when the model is created. Main waits for Felgo's launch
    screen to finish, opens onboarding, and also opens MainPage if registration
    was restored. One NavigationStack owns that history. Home and Statistics
    are tabs inside MainPage, not separate stacks. The supplied loadFuelRecords()
    reads the saved array, treats a missing key as an empty list, and puts read
    errors in fuelRecordsError for the list to display.

    Register Tesla, restart the app, and check that Home still shows Tesla.
    Tap Reset: it clears registration and all fuel records and returns to
    onboarding. Register Tesla again to continue on Home.

    \section1 4. Build a reusable fuel-record list

    Home and Statistics need the same cards, empty message, and error display.
    qml/components/FuelRecordsList.qml already has the outer layout and status
    messages. Its records property is the input; recordClicked is the output.
    The ListView inside it has no delegate yet, so it cannot draw a record.

    Replace that ListView with:
    \quotefromfile qml/components/FuelRecordsList.qml
    \skipto ListView {
    \printuntil /^    \}/

    The model is an array of record objects. ListView creates a delegate for
    each entry and exposes that object as modelData. The first RowLayout puts
    the date and cost on opposite sides; the second line shows mileage and liters.
    Giving the delegate the view's width keeps every card aligned.

    The MouseArea sends the selected object through recordClicked instead of
    opening a page itself. That lets Statistics reuse this component without
    editing: its editable property remains false. Theme.listItem supplies
    the card colors to both tabs. cardProgress changes opacity and translation,
    leaving the layout's final positions intact during the entrance animation.

    In qml/pages/HomePage.qml, replace the empty Item below the recordActions
    RowLayout with:
    \quotefromfile qml/pages/HomePage.qml
    \skipto FuelRecordsList {
    \printuntil /^    \}/

    records binds the model data to the list. errorMessage and emptyDescription
    choose its status text. Passing cardEntrance keeps the list in the same
    animation sequence as the vehicle card and add button.

    Run the app. Home should show the empty-list message below Fuel records.
    To check the cards before connecting storage, temporarily replace HomePage's
    fuelRecords property with this preview array:
    \quotefile docs/snippets/preview-fuel-records.inc

    You should see two cards, with October 20 above October 10. Check the
    date and cost on the first line, then mileage and liters below. These
    records exist only in Home; they do not change DataModel or Storage.
    Tapping a card will not open the form until we connect editing in step 7.

    \raw HTML
    <figure class="screenshot"><a href="images/list-preview.png"><img src="images/list-preview.png" alt="Two temporary fuel-record cards on Home before storage is connected"></a><figcaption>The preview array lets us check the card layout before saving real records.</figcaption></figure>
    \endraw

    Before continuing, restore the model binding so saved records will reach
    the list in step 7:
    \quotefromfile qml/pages/HomePage.qml
    \skipto property var fuelRecords:
    \printuntil property var fuelRecords:

    Run again: the empty-list message should return. The + button already
    sends addFuelRecordRequested; we will connect its handler after building
    the form. The temporary preview array must be gone before that work starts.

    \section1 5. Build the fuel-record form

    qml/pages/AddFuelRecordPage.qml supplies an AppPage with a scrollable form,
    a fixed save bar, and a DatePickerModal. Keeping the save bar outside
    AppFlickable lets the fields scroll while the action stays visible.
    Its bottom margin follows getApplication().keyboardHeight so the keyboard
    can move it out of the way.

    Inside AppFlickable's ColumnLayout, replace the three QtObject field
    placeholders with the following fields, in order. Put the date field first,
    before Odometer, and leave the error label after the fields:
    \quotefromfile qml/pages/AddFuelRecordPage.qml
    \skipto FuelRecordField {
    \printuntil /^      \}/
    \skipto FuelRecordField {
    \printuntil /^      \}/
    \skipto FuelRecordField {
    \printuntil /^      \}/
    \skipto FuelRecordField {
    \printuntil /^      \}/

    FuelRecordField combines a label, a text field, and an error message.
    Its text alias exposes the inner field's value. The inputMethodHints ask
    for a suitable keyboard; they do not validate pasted or typed values.

    Date is read-only because the user selects it in a sheet. openForDate()
    sets the wheel's current date and opens the supplied modal. Its dateSelected
    signal updates selectedDate; the field's text binding then displays the change.
    This keeps a Date value in the page and formats it only for display or saving.

    The page already gathers the values in this draft binding. Read it before
    continuing; do not add a second copy:
    \quotefromfile qml/pages/AddFuelRecordPage.qml
    \skipto readonly property var draft:
    \printuntil /^  \}\)/

    The three number fields still contain strings. The next step will turn
    them into numbers and reject invalid input. For now, keep the supplied
    save bar: its button calls submitRecord(), which we will implement next.

    Main has a fuelRecordComponent containing AddFuelRecordPage. Its success
    signals close the page:
    \quotefromfile qml/Main.qml
    \skipto AddFuelRecordPage {
    \printuntil /^    \}/

    In Main.qml's Connections targeting appNavigation.currentPage, implement
    onAddFuelRecordRequested() so Home's + button opens that component:
    \quotefromfile qml/Main.qml
    \skipto function onAddFuelRecordRequested(
    \printuntil /^    \}/

    Home forwards its request through MainPage. Main owns the stack and pushes
    the form; the form does not need to know which screen opened it.
    Tap + on Home and check all four fields. Open Date, choose another date,
    and tap Done. Reopen the sheet and use Cancel, then the form's back button
    to return to Home. Saving is not connected yet.

    \raw HTML
    <figure class="screenshot"><a href="images/fuel-form.png"><img src="images/fuel-form.png" alt="Fuel form with date, odometer, liters, and cost in euros"></a><figcaption>The fields scroll above the fixed save button. Date opens a separate sheet.</figcaption></figure>
    \endraw

    \section1 6. Validate input before sending an action

    In qml/logic/FuelRecordValidation.js, replace validate(draft). Keep the
    supplied numberText() and isValidDate() helpers:
    \quotefromfile qml/logic/FuelRecordValidation.js
    \skipto function validate(
    \printuntil /^\}/

    A blank field and the number zero need different treatment: Number("")
    returns zero. Checking the trimmed text first prevents a blank odometer
    from passing the numeric check. Mileage and cost may be zero; liters must
    be greater than zero. numberText() also accepts a decimal comma.
    isValidDate() rejects invalid or future dates.

    The result contains valid, errors keyed by draft property, and a record
    with numeric values. The form's errorText bindings use those same keys. They are shown only after isShowValidationErrors becomes true,
    so an untouched form does not greet the user with errors.

    In AddFuelRecordPage.qml, implement submitRecord():
    \quotefromfile qml/pages/AddFuelRecordPage.qml
    \skipto function submitRecord(
    \printuntil /^  \}/

    draft and validation are bindings, so they reflect the latest input.
    The early return keeps invalid data on the form. With valid input, the
    page requests an add or update through Logic. isSaving prevents a second submission;
    the supplied model Connections clear that state on success or failure.

    On Home, tap + and try zero liters or a blank Odometer. The page should
    show the relevant error and stay open. Correct the values and save again:
    the starter model reports that saving is not connected yet. The form
    stays open with your input intact. Go back to Home before continuing.

    \section1 7. Save, edit, and delete records

    DataModel owns the saved array so both tabs observe the same state.

    \section2 Save records

    First implement storeFuelRecords(records) in qml/model/DataModel.qml:
    \quotefromfile qml/model/DataModel.qml
    \skipto function storeFuelRecords(
    \printuntil /^  \}/

    Storage finishes the call before returning. If it emits storageError,
    the supplied handler sets lastStorageError during that call. Update
    fuelRecords only after success, keeping the visible list consistent with
    the saved array. Assigning a new array also notifies QML bindings.

    In the same file, implement saveFuelRecord(draft, recordId):
    \quotefromfile qml/model/DataModel.qml
    \skipto function saveFuelRecord(
    \printuntil /^  \}/

    The model validates again so every caller follows the same rules. slice()
    gives us a working array without changing the displayed list before saving.
    A new record gets an ID; an edit keeps its ID and replaces only its fields.
    Sorting yyyy-MM-dd dates puts the newest record first. The saved signal
    reaches the form, which emits saved so Main can pop it from the stack.

    Add these records from Home's + button:
    \table
      \header \li Date \li Odometer (km) \li Fuel (L) \li Cost (€)
      \row \li 2026-10-10 \li 42000 \li 35.5 \li 62.50
      \row \li 2026-10-20 \li 42400 \li 32 \li 58.40
    \endtable

    Home should show two cards, newest first. Restart and check that both
    remain. If saving fails, check storeFuelRecords() and lastStorageError.

    \section2 Edit an existing record

    To open an existing record, implement onEditFuelRecordRequested(record)
    in the same Main.qml Connections used for adding:
    \quotefromfile qml/Main.qml
    \skipto function onEditFuelRecordRequested(
    \printuntil /^    \}/

    recordToEdit selects edit mode. The supplied Component.onCompleted handler
    fills the fields from that object, and submitRecord() sends an update action.
    The ID identifies the record even if its date or position changes.

    Tap a card on Home, change its cost, save, and reopen it to check the change.
    Restore the sample amount and save again before continuing.

    \section2 Delete a record

    Implement deleteFuelRecord(recordId) in DataModel.qml:
    \quotefromfile qml/model/DataModel.qml
    \skipto function deleteFuelRecord(
    \printuntil /^  \}/

    filter() builds an array without the selected ID. The same storage helper
    saves it before the model announces success. The page already asks for
    confirmation through NativeDialog.confirm(); cancelling keeps the record.
    Its deleted signal closes the page after a successful deletion.

    Add a third record from Home, open it, and confirm its deletion.
    Home should return to the original two records, including after another restart.

    \section1 8. Reuse the records for period totals

    In qml/pages/StatisticsPage.qml, implement filterRecords():
    \quotefromfile qml/pages/StatisticsPage.qml
    \skipto function filterRecords(
    \printuntil /^  \}/

    The date strings use the same yyyy-MM-dd format, so string comparison also
    gives date order. Both selected endpoints are included. filteredRecords
    feeds the shared FuelRecordsList; Statistics leaves editing disabled.
    We save one array of records and derive this view from it.

    Open Statistics and tap All time: both records should appear. Set From to
    2026-10-01 and To to 2026-10-15: only October 10 remains. Set From later
    than To to see the period error, then restore October 1 to 15.
    Totals stay at zero until we implement calculateTotals() in the same file:
    \quotefromfile qml/pages/StatisticsPage.qml
    \skipto function calculateTotals(
    \printuntil /^  \}/

    Sum filteredRecords rather than the full saved array, so the totals match
    the visible cards. The view formats both values to two decimal places.
    Bindings recalculate them when the records or dates change; storing totals
    separately would require keeping another saved value up to date.

    All time should show 2 records, 67.50 L, and 120.90 €. October 1 to 15
    should show 1 record, 35.50 L, and 62.50 €. Predict the totals for October
    16 to 20, then check them. Before reading the solution, try selecting
    October 20 as both endpoints: the record on that date should remain.

    \section1 9. Cover the screen before switching pages

    The car drives away as a circle grows to cover onboarding. We switch to
    Home behind that cover, then reveal it through an expanding circular opening.
    Finally, the cards slide into place.

    Main owns TransitionOverlay above the stack. OnboardingPage receives it
    through a property and requests registration with a signal; Main handles navigation.

    In OnboardingPage.qml, replace the register button's onClicked handler:
    \quotefromfile qml/pages/OnboardingPage.qml
    \skipto onClicked: {
    \printuntil /^      \}/

    carDeparture moves the car while transitionOverlay.start() starts the cover
    animation. Registration waits until the circle covers the screen.
    In the Connections targeting root.transitionOverlay, implement onCovered():
    \quotefromfile qml/pages/OnboardingPage.qml
    \skipto function onCovered()
    \printuntil /^    \}/

    onCovered() sends the registration signal. DataModel saves the name, and
    Main opens MainPage while the screen is covered. After navigation finishes,
    Main calls revealMainPage() to uncover Home. The cards wait until the reveal
    finishes, avoiding a flash of the finished layout before they move.
    If the screen stays covered, check onCovered().

    Keep MainPage's two Qt.callLater calls: they let child bindings and the
    lazily created tab finish initializing before the entrance animation starts.

    Return to Home, tap Reset, and register Tesla again. The car should leave,
    the screen become covered, and Home appear through the circle before the
    cards arrive in order. Try carDeparture's duration at 900 instead of 450,
    compare, then restore 450.

    Your CarLog app now remembers your car and fuel records between launches.
    You can add, edit, and delete records on Home, then use Statistics to see
    how much fuel you bought and what you spent over a selected period.
*/
